So the main activity already has methods for adding and updating inventory.

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:20.890
Each row also receives edit and delete buttons.

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:28.690
Select edit calls, selecting item for editing which remembers the records ID, selected item

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:33.370
ID and copies its values into the input fields.

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:37.730
Updates then uses the ID to identify the record to change.

00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:45.080
The initial value of negative 1 means that no item is selected.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:46.600
Here is a successful operation.

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:50.840
The activity clears the inputs and reloads the inventory grid.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:55.320
This is a working design in the source but it combines several responsibilities in one

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:56.880
class.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:04.120
The same activity builds rows, reads and validates input calls, the database, updates summary

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:11.080
labels, navigates between screens and attempts to send SMS alerts.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:17.360
The concentration of responsibilities is one of the main things that I plan to improve.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:28.410
So the first issue is input validation, not going to be mainactivity.java.

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:35.490
So the first issue is input validation, the method is inventory input valid, checks only

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:40.410
whether the name SKU and quantity text are empty.

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:46.370
It does not check whether the quantity fits the supported integer range, and specifically

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:54.740
we're looking at this right here.

00:06:54.740 --> 00:07:02.660
Both add and update then call integer.parcelent without catching a conversion failure.

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:08.940
A very long number can pass the empty field check but failed during parsing.

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:15.380
The numeric keyboard setting in the layout helps guide ordinary input, it does not replace

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:18.460
validation in the application.

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:27.060
The code also needs an explicit rule against the navigative quantities where every records

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:31.900
can enter the system including feature imports.

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:37.940
There is a related error handling issue when inventory insert returns false, the interface

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:41.100
always says the SKU already exists.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:48.660
When account creation fails, the interface always says the username already exists.

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:54.540
Those are possible causes but the return values do not prove that duplication cause the

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:55.540
failure.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:08:01.580
A more reliable design would distinguish invalid input, duplicate records, and other storage

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:03.580
failures.

