00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:32.380
The notification screen contains another concrete problem, the listener for the deny SMS permission,

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:40.060
total button only changes the status text, it does not revoke operating system permission

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:47.140
or save an application preference that disables alerts.

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:53.340
Next we'll go back into mainactivity.java, it's going to be up here and we'll scroll

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:57.310
down a little bit.

00:08:57.310 --> 00:09:10.170
So if we look at from here to about here, we're looking at the mainactivity.java and

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:17.810
the checks of the actual operating system permissions in a saved phone number.

00:09:17.810 --> 00:09:24.410
This logic does not check the text on the screen as a result.

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:29.210
The interface can say alerts are disabled while the send conditions will still allow

00:09:29.210 --> 00:09:31.010
them.

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:39.090
The SMS method also shows a success message immediately after calling the send text message.

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:45.930
The code does not provide callbacks to confirm sending or delivery and it does not handle exceptions

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:47.970
around that call.

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:54.130
I would separate saving and inventory record from reporting the outcome of an alert.

00:09:54.130 --> 00:10:00.390
A saved record and a delivered text message are different results.

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:06.310
There are smaller usability issues as well, delete acts immediately without confirmation

00:10:06.310 --> 00:10:10.270
and a failed delete has no feedback in this method.

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:16.990
Selecting an item for editing does not explicitly scroll the form into view.

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:23.230
Create navigation starts new activities so I would test the back button behavior instead

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:27.470
of assuming it is intuitive.

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:33.470
Heartcode strings appear throughout the java and layout files which makes consistent wording

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:35.990
and the localization harder.

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:40.990
Finally, the existing automated tests are start examples.

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:46.310
Information checks that 2 plus 2 equals 4 and the other checks the application package

00:10:46.310 --> 00:10:47.310
name.

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:54.030
They do not exercise account handling, inventory validation, updates or notification behavior.

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:59.590
That means the important application rules still need meaningful test coverage.

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:05.630
For documentation and variables, the short comments generally match their code and names

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:10.910
such as selected item ID and database helper explain their purpose.

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:18.070
However, the comments do not adequately describe validation contracts, migration risks or shared

00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:19.990
inventory assumptions.

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:29.670
Formatting is responsible for consistent inspections.

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:34.550
Formal coding standard compliance still needs a link check.

00:11:34.550 --> 00:11:41.670
I did not identify an obviously unused application, variable or unreachable method in this review.

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:49.220
The start tests and unfinished search behavior are the clearer findings.

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:59.140
So for this next part, let's take a look at the logic for our tree over here.

00:11:59.140 --> 00:12:08.180
For this, I'm going to make some of the existing workflows more reliable and easier to maintain.

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:14.420
I will separate stream behavior from validation, inventory rules and persistence.

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:19.780
A repository can provide a consistent way to access inventory while a smaller validation

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:24.820
and business rule classes can be tested without driving the entire Android interface.

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:33.500
I will make quantity validation checks required input numeric conversions, the supported range

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:36.660
and the rule that quantities cannot be negative.

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:43.580
The result should identify the field and explain what the user needs to correct.

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:51.180
Both add and update should use the same validation so their behavior stays consistent.

00:12:51.180 --> 00:12:54.340
Account handling also needs improvement.

00:12:54.340 --> 00:12:58.700
The original code stores passwords directly in a text column.

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:04.700
My plan is to replace plain text password storage with a suitable salted password hashing

00:13:04.700 --> 00:13:09.620
approach and define how existing accounts will transition.

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:15.380
I will also review the current trimming of password input so the app does not silently

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:18.540
change a user's intended password.

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:23.780
I will discuss the database side of that issue later in the review.

00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:32.100
For SMS settings, I plan to represent the user's alert preference, and check it before attempting

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:33.740
to send.

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:38.540
Operating system permission and the user's preference inside the app should be treated

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:40.180
as separate conditions.

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:47.180
I will also make error reporting reflect what the application actually knows about the send

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:49.300
attempt.

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:57.890
Testing will focus on behavior, rejecting an overflow, quantity without crashing, preventing

00:13:57.890 --> 00:14:06.210
an update when no item is selected, preserving a record when deletion is cancelled, and suppressing

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:09.050
an alert when the user disables it.

00:14:09.050 --> 00:14:15.250
I will also test normal operations so the fixes do not break the workflows that already

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:17.530
exist.

00:14:17.530 --> 00:14:24.610
These changes will demonstrate software engineering through separation of concerns, defensive programming,

00:14:24.610 --> 00:14:28.730
maintainable design and evidence from tests.

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:34.010
Explaining the original issue, the design decision and the result will also support clear

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:36.170
technical communication.

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:49.230
My goal is a codebase where a change to support a storage detail or a notification rule does

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:54.630
not require rewriting the inventory screen.

